#!/usr/bin/env perl

use strict;
use FindBin;

BEGIN
{
  require lib;

  foreach my $l
  (
    #? XMRF_LIB will override all other entries
    exists($ENV{XMRF_LIB}) ? $ENV{XMRF_LIB} :
    (
      #? preference order is low to high desc
      !$ENV{XMRF_DEV}
        ? qq[$FindBin::RealBin/../lib]
        : (),
      $FindBin::Bin eq $FindBin::RealBin
        ? qq[$FindBin::Bin/lib]
        : (qq[$FindBin::Bin/lib], qq[$FindBin::RealBin/lib]),
      q[/usr/libexec/xmrf/lib],
      q[/usr/lib/xmrf/lib],
      q[/usr/share/xmrf/lib], #* pref *#
      $ENV{XMRF_DEV}
        ? qq[$FindBin::RealBin/../lib]
        : ()
    )
  )
  { lib->import($l) if -d $l }
}

use xmrf::app;
exit xmrf::app::run();


__END__

=pod

=encoding utf8

=head1 NAME

XMRF - Regex-map Rename File

=head1 SYNOPSIS

 $ xmrf [options] regex sprintf

 --build     | -b          Create output directory structure as-needed.
 --copy      | -c          Use copy instead of move as the action to execute.
 --execute   | -e          Actually perform the actions, safety flag.
 --full      | -f          Pass the full path to regex, not just filename.
 --help      | -h          Show full help dialog.
 --input     | -i <dir>    Scan <dir> for files (and subdirs too with -r).
 --links     | -l          Enable following and renaming symlinks (B<don't>).
 --map       | -m <k>=<v>  Defines a subroutine <v> to run against <k>.
 --named     | -n          Use named capture groups instead of numeric.
 --output    | -o <dir>    Prepend <dir> to output file path.
 --recursive | -r          Scan subfolders of the input directory too.
 --suffix    | -s <str>    Override the default suffix extraction regex.
 --verbose   | -v          Show actions as they happen when execute on.

=head1 DESCRIPTION

B<XMRF> will rename (or copy) files based on an input dir, an output dir, a
regex, and an sprintf format.  It tries to use some sane defaults for things
that aren't provided; at least a regex and sprintf are required to modify files
on the current working directory.

This tool originated as part of another project, B<fairu-chan>, but deserved
its own identity.  It was previously called B<rename>, which is apt but hard
to use due to popularity.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 2

=item B<-b>, B<--build>

After the full output path is generated, create the directory structure as
required with B<File::Path::make_path>.  If in B<--no-build> mode and the full
output directory structure is not already existing, then a warning will be
issued and the current action will be skipped.  It will also warn and then skip
the current action if B<make_path> fails to create the missing directory
structure elements.

=item B<-c>, B<--copy>

Use B<File::Copy::copy> instead of B<File::Copy::move> as the action to perform
on the old and new paths.

=item B<-e>, B<--execute>

Without specifying B<--execute> only the planned actions will be shown.

=item B<-f>, B<--full>

In B<--no-full> mode (B<default>) only the filename (without file extension)
will be passed to the regex.  When the job is complete the new filename will be
prepended with the selected output folder and the extension will be re-added.
The output folder defaults to the input path (with subdirs if -r) if not
provided while in this mode.

If enabled with B<--full>, then the entire path for the file and its extension
will be passed to the regex (sometimes quite useful).  The output folder will
default to nothing if not provided in this mode (even with -r).

=item B<-h>, B<--help>

Show this page.  Use B<-hh> to render the complete POD with B<pod2usage>.  If
installed with a packaged version C<man xmrf> should work as well.

=item B<-i>, B<--input> C<dir>

Change the folder to scan from C<./> to C<dir>.

=item B<-l>, B<--links>

By default B<XMRF> skips over all symlinks (files and directories) for safety.
This behaviour can be changed by setting B<-l>.  It is not recomended and may
have severly unexpected results.

=item B<-m>, B<--map> C<key=val>

Multiple B<--map> flags can be included to specify more mapping subroutines.

Each C<key> is either a numeric (B<--no-named>) or named (B<--named>) captured
group, and each C<val> will be compiled as a subroutine that is given the data
returned by that capture group.  The subroutine should further process the
capture group's value and ultimately return what the sprintf format is
expecting for that slot.

B<NOTE:> Ensure you lexically scope any variables that are declared with C<my>.
If needed, the global C<%config> is available to reference (or modify should
decreased sanity be desired).

If the B<--named> flag is specified then the C<key>s should match the named
capture groups, otherwise they should be numeric (indexed from B<zero>, not
one).  You only have to specify maps for desired keys, not all (in case this
isn't obvious).  Named mode can be useful for specifying the order in which
the output should be populated without needing to resort to C<sprintf>'s
L<format parameter index|https://perldoc.perl.org/functions/sprintf#format-parameter-index>.

  numeric regex: (\d\d).+\[(\w+?)\]          maps: 0='1 + shift', 1='uc shift'
    named regex: (?<b>\d\d).+\[(?<a>\w+?)\]  maps: a='uc shift', b='1 + shift'

 numeric format: %2$s-%1$02d
   named format: %s-%02d

As a quick example (see B<EXAMPLES>), let's add the date to all files in the
current directory:

 $ xmrf '^(.+)()' '%s-%s' -m \
  1='my ($d,$m,$y)=(localtime)[3..5]; sprintf(q[%d-%02d-%02d],$y+1900,$m+1,$d)'

 Info: Planning mode only, no changes will be made...
 mv: './.gitignore' -> './.gitignore-2026-08-06'
 mv: './LICENSE' -> './LICENSE-2026-08-06'

So using an empty capture group additional data can be inserted into the output
using a subroutine.  It may be desirable to modify actual capture data too,
it will be passed as the first argument to the subroutine (C<$_[0]>):

 $ xmrf '^(.+)()' '%s-%s' -m \
  0='uc shift'            -m \
  1='my ($d,$m,$y)=(localtime)[3..5]; sprintf(q[%d-%02d-%02d],$y+1900,$m+1,$d)'

 Info: Planning mode only, no changes will be made...
 mv: './.gitignore' -> './.GITIGNORE-2026-08-06'
 mv: './LICENSE' -> './LICENSE-2026-08-06'

For brevity C<shift> was used, but C<$_[0]> works as well, obviously (and may
be required for more complex situations).

=item B<-n>, B<--named>

In B<--no-named> mode (B<default>) the regex capture groups are taken in order
as an array.  If B<--named> is specified then it is expected the regex will use
named capture groups.  These named capture groups will be sorted with Perl's
C<sort> function when applied to the B<sprintf> section.  Internally this just
means swapping from C<@{^CAPTURE}> to C<%{^CAPTURE}> then applying sort to the
keys.

In numeric mode (B<--no-named>), for the map subroutines, a list of
C<0..$#{^CAPTURE}> is generated to pass as the keys to the map subroutines.
In named mode it's the sorted keys themselves.

=item B<-o>, B<--output> C<dir>

The B<--output> flag, if supplied, will be prepended to the output file from
the B<format> argument.  In B<--no-full> mode, if unspecified, it defaults to
the B<--input> folder (with subdirs if -r).  In B<--full> mode it defaults to
nothing (even with -r).  B<File::Spec> will clean up this path as it sees fit.

Note that the output flag can often be omitted in B<--full> mode, as it's
possible (maybe preferable) to include the output path right in the B<sprintf>
like so:

 $ xmrf -f '.+/(.+?)$' /new/path/to/'%s'

While contrived, this example is equivalent to C<mv * /new/path/to>, or:

 $ xmrf -fo /new/path/to '.+/(.+?)$' '%s'

This idea can also be used to insert some relative paths as well, like:

 $ xmrf '(.+)' relative/path/'%s'

Which would produce paths like C<./relative/path>, because in B<--no-full> mode
the output option would be set to C<./> (same as input) if not provided.

These concepts can be mixed and matched to best suit the goals of the actions
desired.

=item B<-r>, B<--recursive>

If set the input folder and all its subfolders will be scanned for files.

=item B<-s>, B<--suffix> C<str>

The default suffix extraction regex is lazy, taking only the shortest possible
value, i.e. C<item.tar.gz> produces C<gz> as the suffix.  Supply a new regex
with the C<str> provided.  This isn't used in B<--full> mode.  Default regex:

  (?<=.)\.([^.]+)$

=item B<-v>, B<--verbose>

Behaves much like the C<cp> or C<mv> verbose option.  If B<--execute> isn't
enabled then B<--verbose> is forced on to show the planned actions.

=back

=head1 EXAMPLES

=head2 BASIC

=over 2

=item Fix a Long-Standing Typo

 $ xmrf -ri /media '^(.+)HVEC(.+)' '%sHEVC%s'

This keeps everything the same, but fixes a typo in place.

=item Old-School Backups to Modern

 $ xmrf -i /media/backup/show '_(\d+)x(\d+)_' \
   '[DVD Backup] Show - S%02dE%02d [480p]'

Back in the day when we backed up our DVDs we used C<x> between a season
number and episode.

=item Make CRC32s More Appealing

 $ xmrf -i /media/backup/anime ' (\d+(?:v\d)?) .+\[(.+?)\]' \
   '[BluRay Backup] Anime - S01E%s [1080p][%s]' \
   -m 1='uc shift'

Nothing worse than a CRC32 in lowercase.  This one uses a small map-subroutine
on the second capture group.

=back

=head2 ADVANCED

=over 2

=item A Brain Teaser

 $ xmrf -bi ~/Input -o ~/Output \
   '^(\[[\]]+\] (.+?)(?:\.+)?(?: (?:- )?\d+(?i: ?v\d+)?)? (?:\(|\[).+)$' \
   '%2$s/%1$s'

This one inspired L<fairu-chan|https://github.com/justinnamilee/fairu-chan>.

=back

=head1 ENVIRONMENT

B<XMRF> also accepts some options by environment variable.  When loading,
options passed by command-line flag will override anything passed by
environment (except for C<XMRF_DEV> and C<XMRF_LIB>).

=over 2

=item Boolean Environment Variables

The following are boolean (0 or 1) options you can pass by environment instead
of using the option flags.

=over 2

=item * XMRF_BUILD

=item * XMRF_COPY

=item * XMRF_EXECUTE

=item * XMRF_FULL

=item * XMRF_NAMED

=item * XMRF_RECURSIVE

=item * XMRF_VERBOSE

=back

=item String Environment Variables

The following are string options you can pass by environment instead of using
the option flags.

=over 2

=item * XMRF_INPUT

=item * XMRF_OUTPUT

=item * XMRF_SUFFIX

=back

=item Special Environment Variables

=over 2

=item * XMRF_DEV B<(boolean)>

Reorders the library scanner for development.

=item * XMRF_LIB B<(string)>

Skips library scanner and only checks the directory given by this variable.

=back

=back

=head1 INSTALLATION

=head2 With Debian Package

=over 2

=item * Grab the latest C<.deb> release from L<XMRF GitHub|https://github.com/justinnamilee/xmrf/releases/latest>.

=item * Use C<apt> to install it (or whatever).

 $ apt install xmrf_*.deb

=item * Run it!

 $ xmrf

=back

=head2 From Repository

=over 2

=item * Clone the repo.

 $ git clone https://github.com/justinnamilee/xmrf.git

=item * Make sure the version works.

 $ cd xmrf ; prove

=item * Put it somewhere, see C<bin/xmrf> for expected library paths.

Two parts are needed, the wrapper C<bin/xmrf>, and the library
C<lib/xmrf/app.pm>.  Put the wrapper somewhere accessible via C<$PATH>.
The library can go anywhere, but the wrapper has places it will look by
default.  Don't forget to C<chmod +x> the wrapper.

=back

